%I A397339 #19 Aug 23 2026 23:23:10
%S A397339 1,2,5,10,16,23,31,40,50,61,73,86,100,115,131,148,166,185,205,226,248,
%T A397339 271,295,320,346,373,401,430,460,491,523,556,590,625,661,698,736,775,
%U A397339 815,856,898,941,985,1030,1076,1123,1171,1220,1270,1321,1373,1426,1480
%N A397339 Number of distinct triples (i,p,f) arising from a simple graph with n edges, where i, p and f count the edges having 0, 1, and 2 endpoints of degree greater than 1, respectively.
%C A397339 Call (i,p,f) the attachment profile of a graph: classify each edge by how many of its two endpoints carry another edge.
%C A397339 Of the binomial(n+2,2) compositions of n into three nonnegative parts, exactly five fail to be realized for n >= 3, namely (n-1,1,0), (n-1,0,1), (n-2,1,1), (n-2,0,2) and (n-3,1,2).
%C A397339 The first two are impossible because attachment is symmetric: a graph cannot contain exactly one attached edge, since whatever it is attached to is itself attached. The next two hold because two attached edges are attached only to each other, hence each at a single endpoint, so both are partial; a fully attached edge needs two distinct neighbors. The last holds because three mutually attached edges form a star, a path or a triangle, with profiles (3,0), (2,1) and (0,3) respectively - never (1,2).
%C A397339 This sequence agrees with A052905 shifted by two, but the two initial terms are genuinely exceptional rather than a matter of indexing convention. The reason is that the five exclusions above do not all apply at small n: at n = 2 the profile (n-3,1,2) is not a valid triple, leaving a deficit of 4 rather than 5, and at n = 1 the two d = 2 exclusions are invalid as well, leaving a deficit of 2. Concretely a(1) = 1, the single edge, and a(2) = 2, two edges either disjoint or sharing a vertex.
%H A397339 Brian Klemm, <a href="https://brianklemm.com/publications/EdgeAttachmentProfiles.pdf">Edge-Attachment Profiles of Graphs: An Exact Characterization</a>, 2026.
%H A397339 Brian Klemm, <a href="https://brianklemm.com/publications/ega-verify.py">Verification script</a>.
%H A397339 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (3,-3,1).
%F A397339 a(n) = binomial(n+2,2) - 5 for n >= 3.
%F A397339 a(n) = A052905(n-2) for n >= 3.
%F A397339 a(n) = A000217(n+1) - 5 for n >= 3.
%F A397339 a(n) = a(n-1) + n + 1 for n >= 4.
%F A397339 a(n) = 3*a(n-1) - 3*a(n-2) + a(n-3) for n > 5.
%F A397339 G.f.: x*(1 - x + 2*x^2 - x^4)/(1 - x)^3.
%F A397339 E.g.f.: (x^2 + 6*x + 8 + exp(x)*(x^2 + 4*x - 8))/2. - _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 18 2026
%e A397339 For n = 4 the ten profiles, with a witness graph for each:
%e A397339   (4,0,0)  four disjoint edges
%e A397339   (2,2,0)  a path on 3 vertices, plus two disjoint edges
%e A397339   (1,3,0)  star K_{1,3}, plus a disjoint edge
%e A397339   (1,2,1)  path P_4, plus a disjoint edge
%e A397339   (1,0,3)  triangle, plus a disjoint edge
%e A397339   (0,4,0)  star K_{1,4}
%e A397339   (0,3,1)  star K_{1,3} with one edge subdivided
%e A397339   (0,2,2)  path P_5
%e A397339   (0,1,3)  triangle with one pendant edge
%e A397339   (0,0,4)  cycle C_4
%e A397339 The five excluded are (3,1,0), (3,0,1), (2,1,1), (2,0,2) and (1,1,2), and binomial(6,2) - 5 = 10.
%o A397339 (Python)
%o A397339 def a(n): return 1 if n == 1 else 2 if n == 2 else (n+2)*(n+1)//2 - 5
%o A397339 (PARI) a(n) = if(n==1, 1, if(n==2, 2, binomial(n+2,2) - 5));
%Y A397339 Cf. A052905 (this sequence for n >= 3, shifted by two), A000217 (triangular numbers).
%K A397339 nonn,easy,new
%O A397339 1,2
%A A397339 _Brian Klemm_, Aug 16 2026
